Automation Tools (Salesforce)

Automation in Salesforce allows you to implement business logic with minimal or no code (declarative) or custom code (Apex). This section is critical for both interviews and real-world architecture decisions.

8.1 Declarative Automation
Workflow Rules (Legacy)

Definition:
Oldest automation tool in Salesforce used for simple “if this, then that” logic.

Capabilities:

Field Updates
Email Alerts
Task Creation
Outbound Messages

Limitations:

No complex logic (no loops, no branching)
Cannot create records (except tasks)
Being phased out in favor of Flow

Example:

If Opportunity Stage = Closed Won → Send email
Process Builder (Deprecated)

Definition:
A more powerful successor to Workflow Rules, but now deprecated and replaced by Flow.

Capabilities:

Create/update records
Invoke Apex
Post to Chatter
Call Flows

Limitations:

Performance issues (runs slower)
Hard to debug
Salesforce officially recommends migrating to Flow

Example:

When Account Type = Customer → Create related Contact automatically
Flow (MOST IMPORTANT)

Definition:
Flow is the primary automation tool in modern Salesforce. It replaces both Workflow Rules and Process Builder.

Key Features:

Visual drag-and-drop builder
Supports complex logic (loops, decisions)
Can interact with multiple objects
Can call Apex
Can run before/after database operations

Core Components:

Elements: Assignment, Decision, Loop, Get Records, Create Records
Connectors: Define execution path
Variables: Store data

Why Flow is Important:

High performance (especially record-triggered flows)
Full control over logic
Future-proof (Salesforce roadmap focused here)
8.2 Flow Types
1. Record-Triggered Flow

Triggered automatically when a record is created/updated/deleted

Types:

Before Save Flow
Fastest (no DML required)
Used for field updates
After Save Flow
Used for complex logic (create related records, call Apex)

Example:

When Opportunity is created → Auto-create Task
2. Screen Flow

User-interactive flow with UI screens

Used for:

Wizards
Guided data entry
Custom UI processes

Example:

Loan application form with multiple steps
3. Autolaunched Flow

Runs without user interaction, triggered by other processes

Used for:

Sub-flows (called by other flows)
API integrations
Background processes
Called from Apex/Process Builder

Example:

Process Builder → Call Autolaunched Flow → Update related records
4. Scheduled Flow

Runs at a specific time or frequency

Used for:

Batch-like operations
Data cleanup
Scheduled updates

Example:

Every night → Update inactive customers

Flow Categories Summary:
- **Record-Triggered**: Automatic on record changes
- **Screen**: User-guided processes
- **Autolaunched**: Background/API processes
- **Scheduled**: Time-based automation

8.3 When to Use Flow vs Apex

This is one of the most important interview questions.

Use Flow When:
Logic is simple to moderately complex
No heavy computations required
You want low-code / admin-friendly solution
Standard CRUD operations
Maintainability is important

Examples:

Field updates
Record creation
Approval-like logic
Notifications
Use Apex When:
Logic is very complex
Requires loops over large datasets
Needs advanced error handling
Requires integration (API callouts)
Needs transaction control

Examples:

Complex validation across multiple objects
Bulk processing with optimization
External system integrations
Custom algorithms
Flow vs Apex (Comparison Table)
Feature	Flow	Apex
Type	Declarative (No Code)	Programmatic (Code)
Complexity	Medium	High
Performance	Good	Best (if optimized)
Maintainability	Easy	Requires developers
Debugging	Moderate	Advanced
Bulk Handling	Limited	Strong
UI Support	Yes (Screen Flow)	No
Decision Strategy (Golden Rule)
Start with Flow
Move to Apex only if Flow cannot handle the requirement
Real-World Architecture Insight

In enterprise projects:

80% automation → Flow
20% → Apex (for edge cases)

A common pattern:

Flow handles orchestration
Apex handles complex logic